{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-core"},{"type":"text","value":" ships no cron daemon. The shape every mature workflow\nengine converges on is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"external scheduler + durable store + bounded wake-up"}]},{"type":"text","value":".\nThe JavaScript engine should not stay alive just because a workflow is waiting."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Workflow now has two scheduling layers:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-core"},{"type":"text","value":": low-level engine recipes where you bring your own\nscheduler and call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runWorkflow"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-runtime"},{"type":"text","value":": registered schedules, timer indexes, schedule\nmaterialization, and bounded "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.sweep()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the production runtime model, start with the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guide/index.md#wake-timers-and-schedules","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scheduling section in the Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nand the "},{"type":"link","href":"../guide/deployment.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deployment guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":". This page keeps the\nlow-level core recipes because they are still useful when embedding the engine\ndirectly."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-runtime-model-in-one-paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime model in one paragraph"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You register schedules in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineWorkflowRuntime"},{"type":"text","value":". A host cron or scheduled\nfunction calls the runtime sweep. The sweep materializes due schedules, starts\nfresh deterministic runs for due schedule buckets, claims due timers, and resumes\nsleeping workflows by delivering the internal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"__timer"},{"type":"text","value":" signal. The store is the\nsource of truth; cron is only a wake-up tick."}]},{"type":"code","value":"await workflowRuntime.sweep({\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-core-model-in-one-paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core model in one paragraph"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You declare a normal workflow. Something outside the engine (cron daemon,\nCloudflare Cron Trigger, Railway Cron Job, Netlify Scheduled Function,\nEventBridge rule, a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setInterval"},{"type":"text","value":" in a worker, Vercel Cron Job, anything) fires\non schedule. Each tick calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runWorkflow({ workflow, input, runStore })"},{"type":"text","value":" with\nfresh input. The workflow runs end-to-end and finishes; the next tick is a new\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":". No \"loop forever with sleep\" — log doesn't grow, replay cost is\nconstant, and \"when's the next run?\" is answerable from the scheduler, not from\nthe engine."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"recipe-a-node-process-with-node-cron","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recipe: a node process with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"node-cron"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import cron from 'node-cron'\nimport { runWorkflow, inMemoryRunStore } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from './workflows/daily-report'\n\nconst runStore = inMemoryRunStore() // swap for a durable adapter in prod\n\n// 09:00 every Monday in UTC\ncron.schedule(\n  '0 9 * * MON',\n  async () => {\n    for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n      workflow: dailyReport,\n      input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n      runStore,\n    })) {\n      // events flow through here — forward to Redis, log, ignore\n    }\n  },\n  { timezone: 'UTC' },\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The workflow body itself is a normal one-shot:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export const dailyReport = createWorkflow({\n  id: 'daily-report',\n  input: z.object({ triggeredAt: z.number() }),\n}).handler(async (ctx) => {\n  const report = await ctx.step('gen', generateReport)\n  await ctx.step('email', () => emailReport(report))\n  return { ranAt: ctx.input.triggeredAt, report: report.summary }\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"recipe-cloudflare-worker-cron-trigger","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recipe: Cloudflare Worker cron trigger"}]},{"type":"code","value":"# wrangler.toml\n[[triggers.crons]]\ncron = \"0 9 * * MON\"","lang":"toml"},{"type":"code","value":"// worker.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from './workflows/daily-report'\nimport { d1RunStore } from './storage' // hypothetical D1-backed store\n\nexport default {\n  async scheduled(event: ScheduledEvent, env: Env) {\n    const runStore = d1RunStore(env.DB)\n    for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n      workflow: dailyReport,\n      input: { triggeredAt: event.scheduledTime },\n      runStore,\n    })) {\n      /* forward / log */\n    }\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"recipe-railway-cron-job-running-a-command","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recipe: Railway Cron Job running a command"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// scripts/daily-report.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '../workflows/daily-report'\nimport { runStore } from '../storage'\n\nfor await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n  workflow: dailyReport,\n  input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n  runStore,\n})) {\n  /* log / forward */\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure the service's cron schedule in Railway and make the command exit after\nthe workflow reaches completion or its next pause."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"recipe-netlify-scheduled-function","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recipe: Netlify Scheduled Function"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// netlify/functions/daily-report.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '../../src/workflows/daily-report'\nimport { runStore } from '../../src/storage'\n\nexport async function handler() {\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore,\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n  return { statusCode: 200, body: 'ok' }\n}\n\nexport const config = {\n  schedule: '0 9 * * MON',\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"recipe-aws-eventbridge-lambda","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recipe: AWS EventBridge → Lambda"}]},{"type":"code","value":"# serverless.yml (excerpt)\nfunctions:\n  dailyReport:\n    handler: handlers/daily-report.handler\n    events:\n      - schedule: cron(0 9 ? * MON *) # EventBridge cron syntax — UTC","lang":"yaml"},{"type":"code","value":"// handlers/daily-report.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '../workflows/daily-report'\nimport { dynamoRunStore } from '../storage'\n\nexport const handler = async () => {\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore: dynamoRunStore(),\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"recipe-vercel-cron-job-hitting-a-route","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recipe: Vercel Cron Job hitting a route"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// vercel.json\n{\n  \"crons\": [{ \"path\": \"/api/cron/daily-report\", \"schedule\": \"0 9 * * MON\" }]\n}","lang":"jsonc"},{"type":"code","value":"// app/api/cron/daily-report/route.ts (App Router) or pages/api/cron/...\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '@/workflows/daily-report'\nimport { runStore } from '@/lib/run-store'\n\nexport async function GET(req: Request) {\n  // Vercel signs the request; verify the secret before running\n  if (req.headers.get('authorization') !== `Bearer ${process.env.CRON_SECRET}`) {\n    return new Response('unauthorized', { status: 401 })\n  }\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore,\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n  return new Response('ok')\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"recipe-skip-overlap-policy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recipe: skip-overlap policy"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most schedulers ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cron"},{"type":"text","value":", EventBridge, Vercel) don't know whether the previous\ntick is still running. If you want \"skip the new tick if the previous one is\nstill in flight,\" gate on a marker in the run store before starting:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"async function tick() {\n  // Use a deterministic runId so concurrent ticks can't both create one.\n  const runId = `daily-report:${new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10)}` // one per day\n  const existing = await runStore.getRunState(runId)\n  if (existing && existing.status !== 'finished' && existing.status !== 'errored') {\n    return // previous tick still running, skip\n  }\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    runId,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore,\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The engine's start-path idempotency check (same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" + same workflow fingerprint = attach-snapshot, not double-start) means a second-of-two concurrent calls degrades to read-only safely even if the gate above races."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"recipe-buffer-one-policy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recipe: buffer-one policy"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want \"if a tick fires while one is running, run it again as soon as the previous finishes,\" queue locally:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"let pending = false\nlet inFlight: Promise<void> | null = null\n\nasync function tick() {\n  if (inFlight) {\n    pending = true\n    return\n  }\n  inFlight = (async () => {\n    for await (const _ of runWorkflow({ workflow: dailyReport, input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() }, runStore })) {\n      /* … */\n    }\n  })().finally(async () => {\n    inFlight = null\n    if (pending) {\n      pending = false\n      await tick()\n    }\n  })\n  await inFlight\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Holds at most one pending tick — extra ticks during a long run collapse into a single follow-up."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"recipe-writing-your-own-schedule-store","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recipe: writing your own schedule store"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For shops that don't have a host-managed cron, run a tiny worker that polls a schedule table:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface Schedule {\n  id: string\n  workflowId: string\n  cronExpr: string\n  nextFireAt: number\n  inputBuilder: () => unknown\n  overlapPolicy: 'skip' | 'buffer' | 'allow'\n}\n\nasync function tickAllSchedules(schedules: Array<Schedule>, runStore: RunStore) {\n  const now = Date.now()\n  for (const s of schedules) {\n    if (s.nextFireAt > now) continue\n    await fireOne(s, runStore)\n    s.nextFireAt = computeNext(s.cronExpr, now) // use a cron-parser lib\n  }\n}\n\n// Long-running worker\nsetInterval(() => tickAllSchedules(schedules, runStore), 30_000)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A more durable version persists "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nextFireAt"},{"type":"text","value":" alongside each schedule definition;\na deeper one claims due ticks atomically so duplicate schedulers do not start the\nsame work twice. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-runtime"},{"type":"text","value":" provides that newer shape through\nregistered schedules, schedule buckets, leases, and bounded sweeps. See the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guide/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the current production path."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"test-pattern","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Test pattern"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The repo's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tests/examples.external-cron.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" exercises this end-to-end with vitest fake timers — a deterministic scheduler driving multiple ticks against the engine, verifying each tick produces an independent run and that skip-overlap works."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"the-runtime-model-in-one-paragraph","text":"The runtime model in one paragraph","level":2},{"id":"the-core-model-in-one-paragraph","text":"The core model in one paragraph","level":2},{"id":"recipe-a-node-process-with-node-cron","text":"Recipe: a node process with node-cron","level":2},{"id":"recipe-cloudflare-worker-cron-trigger","text":"Recipe: Cloudflare Worker cron trigger","level":2},{"id":"recipe-railway-cron-job-running-a-command","text":"Recipe: Railway Cron Job running a command","level":2},{"id":"recipe-netlify-scheduled-function","text":"Recipe: Netlify Scheduled Function","level":2},{"id":"recipe-aws-eventbridge-lambda","text":"Recipe: AWS EventBridge → Lambda","level":2},{"id":"recipe-vercel-cron-job-hitting-a-route","text":"Recipe: Vercel Cron Job hitting a route","level":2},{"id":"recipe-skip-overlap-policy","text":"Recipe: skip-overlap policy","level":2},{"id":"recipe-buffer-one-policy","text":"Recipe: buffer-one policy","level":2},{"id":"recipe-writing-your-own-schedule-store","text":"Recipe: writing your own schedule store","level":2},{"id":"test-pattern","text":"Test pattern","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n`@tanstack/workflow-core` ships no cron daemon. The shape every mature workflow\nengine converges on is **external scheduler + durable store + bounded wake-up**.\nThe JavaScript engine should not stay alive just because a workflow is waiting.\n\nTanStack Workflow now has two scheduling layers:\n\n- `@tanstack/workflow-core`: low-level engine recipes where you bring your own\n  scheduler and call `runWorkflow`.\n- `@tanstack/workflow-runtime`: registered schedules, timer indexes, schedule\n  materialization, and bounded `runtime.sweep()` calls.\n\nFor the production runtime model, start with the\n[Scheduling section in the Guide](../guide/index.md#wake-timers-and-schedules)\nand the [Deployment guide](../guide/deployment.md). This page keeps the\nlow-level core recipes because they are still useful when embedding the engine\ndirectly.\n\n## The runtime model in one paragraph\n\nYou register schedules in `defineWorkflowRuntime`. A host cron or scheduled\nfunction calls the runtime sweep. The sweep materializes due schedules, starts\nfresh deterministic runs for due schedule buckets, claims due timers, and resumes\nsleeping workflows by delivering the internal `__timer` signal. The store is the\nsource of truth; cron is only a wake-up tick.\n\n```ts\nawait workflowRuntime.sweep({\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})\n```\n\n## The core model in one paragraph\n\nYou declare a normal workflow. Something outside the engine (cron daemon,\nCloudflare Cron Trigger, Railway Cron Job, Netlify Scheduled Function,\nEventBridge rule, a `setInterval` in a worker, Vercel Cron Job, anything) fires\non schedule. Each tick calls `runWorkflow({ workflow, input, runStore })` with\nfresh input. The workflow runs end-to-end and finishes; the next tick is a new\n`runId`. No \"loop forever with sleep\" — log doesn't grow, replay cost is\nconstant, and \"when's the next run?\" is answerable from the scheduler, not from\nthe engine.\n\n## Recipe: a node process with `node-cron`\n\n```ts\nimport cron from 'node-cron'\nimport { runWorkflow, inMemoryRunStore } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from './workflows/daily-report'\n\nconst runStore = inMemoryRunStore() // swap for a durable adapter in prod\n\n// 09:00 every Monday in UTC\ncron.schedule(\n  '0 9 * * MON',\n  async () => {\n    for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n      workflow: dailyReport,\n      input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n      runStore,\n    })) {\n      // events flow through here — forward to Redis, log, ignore\n    }\n  },\n  { timezone: 'UTC' },\n)\n```\n\nThe workflow body itself is a normal one-shot:\n\n```ts\nexport const dailyReport = createWorkflow({\n  id: 'daily-report',\n  input: z.object({ triggeredAt: z.number() }),\n}).handler(async (ctx) => {\n  const report = await ctx.step('gen', generateReport)\n  await ctx.step('email', () => emailReport(report))\n  return { ranAt: ctx.input.triggeredAt, report: report.summary }\n})\n```\n\n## Recipe: Cloudflare Worker cron trigger\n\n```toml\n# wrangler.toml\n[[triggers.crons]]\ncron = \"0 9 * * MON\"\n```\n\n```ts\n// worker.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from './workflows/daily-report'\nimport { d1RunStore } from './storage' // hypothetical D1-backed store\n\nexport default {\n  async scheduled(event: ScheduledEvent, env: Env) {\n    const runStore = d1RunStore(env.DB)\n    for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n      workflow: dailyReport,\n      input: { triggeredAt: event.scheduledTime },\n      runStore,\n    })) {\n      /* forward / log */\n    }\n  },\n}\n```\n\n## Recipe: Railway Cron Job running a command\n\n```ts\n// scripts/daily-report.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '../workflows/daily-report'\nimport { runStore } from '../storage'\n\nfor await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n  workflow: dailyReport,\n  input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n  runStore,\n})) {\n  /* log / forward */\n}\n```\n\nConfigure the service's cron schedule in Railway and make the command exit after\nthe workflow reaches completion or its next pause.\n\n## Recipe: Netlify Scheduled Function\n\n```ts\n// netlify/functions/daily-report.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '../../src/workflows/daily-report'\nimport { runStore } from '../../src/storage'\n\nexport async function handler() {\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore,\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n  return { statusCode: 200, body: 'ok' }\n}\n\nexport const config = {\n  schedule: '0 9 * * MON',\n}\n```\n\n## Recipe: AWS EventBridge → Lambda\n\n```yaml\n# serverless.yml (excerpt)\nfunctions:\n  dailyReport:\n    handler: handlers/daily-report.handler\n    events:\n      - schedule: cron(0 9 ? * MON *) # EventBridge cron syntax — UTC\n```\n\n```ts\n// handlers/daily-report.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '../workflows/daily-report'\nimport { dynamoRunStore } from '../storage'\n\nexport const handler = async () => {\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore: dynamoRunStore(),\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Recipe: Vercel Cron Job hitting a route\n\n```jsonc\n// vercel.json\n{\n  \"crons\": [{ \"path\": \"/api/cron/daily-report\", \"schedule\": \"0 9 * * MON\" }]\n}\n```\n\n```ts\n// app/api/cron/daily-report/route.ts (App Router) or pages/api/cron/...\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '@/workflows/daily-report'\nimport { runStore } from '@/lib/run-store'\n\nexport async function GET(req: Request) {\n  // Vercel signs the request; verify the secret before running\n  if (req.headers.get('authorization') !== `Bearer ${process.env.CRON_SECRET}`) {\n    return new Response('unauthorized', { status: 401 })\n  }\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore,\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n  return new Response('ok')\n}\n```\n\n## Recipe: skip-overlap policy\n\nMost schedulers (`cron`, EventBridge, Vercel) don't know whether the previous\ntick is still running. If you want \"skip the new tick if the previous one is\nstill in flight,\" gate on a marker in the run store before starting:\n\n```ts\nasync function tick() {\n  // Use a deterministic runId so concurrent ticks can't both create one.\n  const runId = `daily-report:${new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10)}` // one per day\n  const existing = await runStore.getRunState(runId)\n  if (existing && existing.status !== 'finished' && existing.status !== 'errored') {\n    return // previous tick still running, skip\n  }\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    runId,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore,\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe engine's start-path idempotency check (same `runId` + same workflow fingerprint = attach-snapshot, not double-start) means a second-of-two concurrent calls degrades to read-only safely even if the gate above races.\n\n## Recipe: buffer-one policy\n\nIf you want \"if a tick fires while one is running, run it again as soon as the previous finishes,\" queue locally:\n\n```ts\nlet pending = false\nlet inFlight: Promise<void> | null = null\n\nasync function tick() {\n  if (inFlight) {\n    pending = true\n    return\n  }\n  inFlight = (async () => {\n    for await (const _ of runWorkflow({ workflow: dailyReport, input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() }, runStore })) {\n      /* … */\n    }\n  })().finally(async () => {\n    inFlight = null\n    if (pending) {\n      pending = false\n      await tick()\n    }\n  })\n  await inFlight\n}\n```\n\nHolds at most one pending tick — extra ticks during a long run collapse into a single follow-up.\n\n## Recipe: writing your own schedule store\n\nFor shops that don't have a host-managed cron, run a tiny worker that polls a schedule table:\n\n```ts\ninterface Schedule {\n  id: string\n  workflowId: string\n  cronExpr: string\n  nextFireAt: number\n  inputBuilder: () => unknown\n  overlapPolicy: 'skip' | 'buffer' | 'allow'\n}\n\nasync function tickAllSchedules(schedules: Array<Schedule>, runStore: RunStore) {\n  const now = Date.now()\n  for (const s of schedules) {\n    if (s.nextFireAt > now) continue\n    await fireOne(s, runStore)\n    s.nextFireAt = computeNext(s.cronExpr, now) // use a cron-parser lib\n  }\n}\n\n// Long-running worker\nsetInterval(() => tickAllSchedules(schedules, runStore), 30_000)\n```\n\nA more durable version persists `nextFireAt` alongside each schedule definition;\na deeper one claims due ticks atomically so duplicate schedulers do not start the\nsame work twice. `@tanstack/workflow-runtime` provides that newer shape through\nregistered schedules, schedule buckets, leases, and bounded sweeps. See the\n[Guide](../guide/index.md) for the current production path.\n\n## Test pattern\n\nThe repo's `tests/examples.external-cron.test.ts` exercises this end-to-end with vitest fake timers — a deterministic scheduler driving multiple ticks against the engine, verifying each tick produces an independent run and that skip-overlap works.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: scheduling\ntitle: Scheduling and Recurring Runs\n---\n\n`@tanstack/workflow-core` ships no cron daemon. The shape every mature workflow\nengine converges on is **external scheduler + durable store + bounded wake-up**.\nThe JavaScript engine should not stay alive just because a workflow is waiting.\n\nTanStack Workflow now has two scheduling layers:\n\n- `@tanstack/workflow-core`: low-level engine recipes where you bring your own\n  scheduler and call `runWorkflow`.\n- `@tanstack/workflow-runtime`: registered schedules, timer indexes, schedule\n  materialization, and bounded `runtime.sweep()` calls.\n\nFor the production runtime model, start with the\n[Scheduling section in the Guide](../guide/index.md#wake-timers-and-schedules)\nand the [Deployment guide](../guide/deployment.md). This page keeps the\nlow-level core recipes because they are still useful when embedding the engine\ndirectly.\n\n## The runtime model in one paragraph\n\nYou register schedules in `defineWorkflowRuntime`. A host cron or scheduled\nfunction calls the runtime sweep. The sweep materializes due schedules, starts\nfresh deterministic runs for due schedule buckets, claims due timers, and resumes\nsleeping workflows by delivering the internal `__timer` signal. The store is the\nsource of truth; cron is only a wake-up tick.\n\n```ts\nawait workflowRuntime.sweep({\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})\n```\n\n## The core model in one paragraph\n\nYou declare a normal workflow. Something outside the engine (cron daemon,\nCloudflare Cron Trigger, Railway Cron Job, Netlify Scheduled Function,\nEventBridge rule, a `setInterval` in a worker, Vercel Cron Job, anything) fires\non schedule. Each tick calls `runWorkflow({ workflow, input, runStore })` with\nfresh input. The workflow runs end-to-end and finishes; the next tick is a new\n`runId`. No \"loop forever with sleep\" — log doesn't grow, replay cost is\nconstant, and \"when's the next run?\" is answerable from the scheduler, not from\nthe engine.\n\n## Recipe: a node process with `node-cron`\n\n```ts\nimport cron from 'node-cron'\nimport { runWorkflow, inMemoryRunStore } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from './workflows/daily-report'\n\nconst runStore = inMemoryRunStore() // swap for a durable adapter in prod\n\n// 09:00 every Monday in UTC\ncron.schedule(\n  '0 9 * * MON',\n  async () => {\n    for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n      workflow: dailyReport,\n      input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n      runStore,\n    })) {\n      // events flow through here — forward to Redis, log, ignore\n    }\n  },\n  { timezone: 'UTC' },\n)\n```\n\nThe workflow body itself is a normal one-shot:\n\n```ts\nexport const dailyReport = createWorkflow({\n  id: 'daily-report',\n  input: z.object({ triggeredAt: z.number() }),\n}).handler(async (ctx) => {\n  const report = await ctx.step('gen', generateReport)\n  await ctx.step('email', () => emailReport(report))\n  return { ranAt: ctx.input.triggeredAt, report: report.summary }\n})\n```\n\n## Recipe: Cloudflare Worker cron trigger\n\n```toml\n# wrangler.toml\n[[triggers.crons]]\ncron = \"0 9 * * MON\"\n```\n\n```ts\n// worker.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from './workflows/daily-report'\nimport { d1RunStore } from './storage' // hypothetical D1-backed store\n\nexport default {\n  async scheduled(event: ScheduledEvent, env: Env) {\n    const runStore = d1RunStore(env.DB)\n    for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n      workflow: dailyReport,\n      input: { triggeredAt: event.scheduledTime },\n      runStore,\n    })) {\n      /* forward / log */\n    }\n  },\n}\n```\n\n## Recipe: Railway Cron Job running a command\n\n```ts\n// scripts/daily-report.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '../workflows/daily-report'\nimport { runStore } from '../storage'\n\nfor await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n  workflow: dailyReport,\n  input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n  runStore,\n})) {\n  /* log / forward */\n}\n```\n\nConfigure the service's cron schedule in Railway and make the command exit after\nthe workflow reaches completion or its next pause.\n\n## Recipe: Netlify Scheduled Function\n\n```ts\n// netlify/functions/daily-report.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '../../src/workflows/daily-report'\nimport { runStore } from '../../src/storage'\n\nexport async function handler() {\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore,\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n  return { statusCode: 200, body: 'ok' }\n}\n\nexport const config = {\n  schedule: '0 9 * * MON',\n}\n```\n\n## Recipe: AWS EventBridge → Lambda\n\n```yaml\n# serverless.yml (excerpt)\nfunctions:\n  dailyReport:\n    handler: handlers/daily-report.handler\n    events:\n      - schedule: cron(0 9 ? * MON *) # EventBridge cron syntax — UTC\n```\n\n```ts\n// handlers/daily-report.ts\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '../workflows/daily-report'\nimport { dynamoRunStore } from '../storage'\n\nexport const handler = async () => {\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore: dynamoRunStore(),\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Recipe: Vercel Cron Job hitting a route\n\n```jsonc\n// vercel.json\n{\n  \"crons\": [{ \"path\": \"/api/cron/daily-report\", \"schedule\": \"0 9 * * MON\" }]\n}\n```\n\n```ts\n// app/api/cron/daily-report/route.ts (App Router) or pages/api/cron/...\nimport { runWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { dailyReport } from '@/workflows/daily-report'\nimport { runStore } from '@/lib/run-store'\n\nexport async function GET(req: Request) {\n  // Vercel signs the request; verify the secret before running\n  if (req.headers.get('authorization') !== `Bearer ${process.env.CRON_SECRET}`) {\n    return new Response('unauthorized', { status: 401 })\n  }\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore,\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n  return new Response('ok')\n}\n```\n\n## Recipe: skip-overlap policy\n\nMost schedulers (`cron`, EventBridge, Vercel) don't know whether the previous\ntick is still running. If you want \"skip the new tick if the previous one is\nstill in flight,\" gate on a marker in the run store before starting:\n\n```ts\nasync function tick() {\n  // Use a deterministic runId so concurrent ticks can't both create one.\n  const runId = `daily-report:${new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10)}` // one per day\n  const existing = await runStore.getRunState(runId)\n  if (existing && existing.status !== 'finished' && existing.status !== 'errored') {\n    return // previous tick still running, skip\n  }\n  for await (const _ of runWorkflow({\n    workflow: dailyReport,\n    runId,\n    input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() },\n    runStore,\n  })) {\n    /* … */\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe engine's start-path idempotency check (same `runId` + same workflow fingerprint = attach-snapshot, not double-start) means a second-of-two concurrent calls degrades to read-only safely even if the gate above races.\n\n## Recipe: buffer-one policy\n\nIf you want \"if a tick fires while one is running, run it again as soon as the previous finishes,\" queue locally:\n\n```ts\nlet pending = false\nlet inFlight: Promise<void> | null = null\n\nasync function tick() {\n  if (inFlight) {\n    pending = true\n    return\n  }\n  inFlight = (async () => {\n    for await (const _ of runWorkflow({ workflow: dailyReport, input: { triggeredAt: Date.now() }, runStore })) {\n      /* … */\n    }\n  })().finally(async () => {\n    inFlight = null\n    if (pending) {\n      pending = false\n      await tick()\n    }\n  })\n  await inFlight\n}\n```\n\nHolds at most one pending tick — extra ticks during a long run collapse into a single follow-up.\n\n## Recipe: writing your own schedule store\n\nFor shops that don't have a host-managed cron, run a tiny worker that polls a schedule table:\n\n```ts\ninterface Schedule {\n  id: string\n  workflowId: string\n  cronExpr: string\n  nextFireAt: number\n  inputBuilder: () => unknown\n  overlapPolicy: 'skip' | 'buffer' | 'allow'\n}\n\nasync function tickAllSchedules(schedules: Array<Schedule>, runStore: RunStore) {\n  const now = Date.now()\n  for (const s of schedules) {\n    if (s.nextFireAt > now) continue\n    await fireOne(s, runStore)\n    s.nextFireAt = computeNext(s.cronExpr, now) // use a cron-parser lib\n  }\n}\n\n// Long-running worker\nsetInterval(() => tickAllSchedules(schedules, runStore), 30_000)\n```\n\nA more durable version persists `nextFireAt` alongside each schedule definition;\na deeper one claims due ticks atomically so duplicate schedulers do not start the\nsame work twice. `@tanstack/workflow-runtime` provides that newer shape through\nregistered schedules, schedule buckets, leases, and bounded sweeps. See the\n[Guide](../guide/index.md) for the current production path.\n\n## Test pattern\n\nThe repo's `tests/examples.external-cron.test.ts` exercises this end-to-end with vitest fake timers — a deterministic scheduler driving multiple ticks against the engine, verifying each tick produces an independent run and that skip-overlap works.\n"}
